  <page_number>72</page_number>
  <running_head>I. IACOMOTI</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>MAR-</catchword>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <l>Qui Syracusias celebris arces</l>
    <l>Texit, atque diu nouis potentes</l>
    <l>Luísit artibus Italûm cateruas:</l>
    <l>Nostri Dedalea manus Duranti</l>
    <l>Quàm grato variant labore chartas,</l>
    <l>Quàm mutant varijs typos figuris.</l>
    <l>Prata sic variant suos colores:</l>
    <l>Flores sic variant suos odores.</l>
    <l>Ten' Chiragra feris nocere flagris</l>
    <l>Atque audire truces iuuat querelas?</l>
    <l>Inuisis lapidosa tunde nodis</l>
    <l>Pigros articulos, manúsque inertes.</l>
    <l>Desidum, licet, otiosa ventrum</l>
    <l>Cæde pondera tristibus flagellis,</l>
    <l>Qui sub Santonico cutem culullo</l>
    <l>Curant molliculam atque delicatam:</l>
    <l>Et quos Ganea, quos popina pernox</l>
    <l>Nudat lucifugos, tenebriones.</l>
    <l>Manus sanguineas preme, &amp; nocentes,</l>
    <l>Auaras, rapidas, &amp; impudicas</l>
    <l>Quæ tam spurca suis notant libellis,</l>
    <l>Tam turpes Veneres Cupidinésque.</l>
    <l>Istis si nequeas satis nocere,</l>
    <l>Pedes excruciet simul Podagra.</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The catchword "MAR-" is a half word at the lower right. A printed ornament sits below the text and is not transcribed. Faint show-through from the reverse side of the leaf is ignored.</notes>
</page>
